From 14906602a135fd37f19570dfee3dddcf63db6e75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:45:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: Dolby Vision in a mode named by its HDMI_VIC, with the
 CTA VIC in the AVI InfoFrame

In a mode that NVKMS signals by its HDMI_VIC (3840x2160 at 24, 25 and 30 Hz
and 4096x2160 at 24 Hz from the HDMI VSDB of a display without UHD_VIC, their
1000/1001 rates too) the AVI InfoFrame carries VIC 0 and NVKMS's own vendor
InfoFrame names the format. A client's Dolby vendor InfoFrame goes out in the
vendor-specific slot in place of that one, so nvHadesVsifRefusal() refused
the mode set ("the mode needs NVKMS's own HDMI vendor InfoFrame (an HDMI VIC
or 3D)"): a GTX 1060 with an LG TV (a Dolby Vision VSVDB of version 1, 12
bytes) never had Dolby Vision at 3840x2160 at 23.976 Hz, through an AVR or
straight to the TV (275 refusals in one video), and Kodi went on in HDR10.

There the AVI InfoFrame now carries the CTA VIC of the format (95, 94 and 93
for 3840x2160 at 30, 25 and 24 Hz, 98 for 4096x2160 at 24 Hz, with no picture
aspect for 98), as 9062 does for HDR10+, as Hades' 615 does it
(nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9053), as Linux names these formats to an HDMI 2.0
sink and as NVKMS itself names them to a display that says UHD_VIC; NVKMS's own
vendor InfoFrame goes out in no slot while it does, so that the AVI InfoFrame
and a vendor InfoFrame never name two formats; and the mode set is no longer
refused for it (nvHadesDolbyAviCtaVic()). Only:
- with the client's Dolby vendor InfoFrame (IEEE OUI 00-D0-46): the Dolby
  Vision signalling of HDMI 1.4b (9058) names the HDMI_VIC in its own
  InfoFrame and keeps doing so, and a frame-packed 3D mode keeps its own
  vendor InfoFrame (refused as before);
- to an HDMI 2.0 sink (SCDC_Present in its HF-VSDB) that lists that CTA VIC
  in a video data block, the sink of the rule of 9062;
- kept as 9062 keeps it: where the AVI InfoFrame of the head carries that CTA
  VIC already in the same mode and the display declares Dolby Vision (a Dolby
  Vision VSVDB that NVKMS parsed), the end of Dolby Vision changes nothing of
  the signalling until the next change of mode.
Elsewhere the mode set is refused as before, and the kernel log now says which
of the two the display lacks ("Hades: the HDMI vendor InfoFrame of head N is
refused: the mode needs NVKMS's own HDMI vendor InfoFrame (it names the mode by
its HDMI_VIC), and the display is not HDMI 2.0 (no SCDC_Present in its
HF-VSDB) ..." / "... and the display lists no CTA VIC of that format in its
video data blocks ..."). When the CTA VIC starts, the line of 9062 names Dolby
Vision: "Hades: head N: the AVI InfoFrame says VIC V for the format of the
mode, which NVKMS names by its HDMI_VIC, because the Dolby Vision vendor
InfoFrame takes the place of NVKMS's own (which goes out in no slot while the
AVI InfoFrame says it)".

The video data block test of 9062 is shared with the mode set check
(nvHadesVdbListsVic()), so that the check and the InfoFrames follow one rule.

Not run on a GPU; a Maxwell, Pascal or Volta GPU and a Dolby Vision display
without UHD_VIC at 4K 24, 25 or 30 Hz are what can show it.

Checked with the host test of 9062 (nvUpdateHdmiInfoFrames() of the tree with
the HAL of these GPUs and the HDMI packet library on a register file) extended
with Dolby Vision steps and three displays made from the EDID of a Samsung
S90H without UHD_VIC with a Dolby Vision VSVDB (as it is, without SCDC,
without VIC 93), and with a test of nvHadesVsifRefusal() and
nvHadesDolbyAviCtaVic() over all their inputs against an oracle; against the
tree before, every step outside Dolby Vision in such a mode the same.
